59 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (The IMF's latest World Economic Outlook revised global growth down to 3.1% for 2026 and pushed global headline inflation up to 4.4% - both attributable to the ongoing Middle East conflict and its pass-through into energy and commodity prices \(IMF World Economic Outlook, April 2026\). In the US, core PCE remains above target and the Fed remains on hold; in Europe, the ECB faces a more complex trade-off between slowing growth and an externally driven inflation shock. Equity markets were volatile, credit spreads widened in leveraged segments, and investor appetite shifted toward shorter-duration, lower-volatility strategies. Against this backdrop, the Fund's performance remained stable and uncorrelated - a direct reflection of the structural insulation offered by short-duration, self- liquidating assets with no exposure to rate-sensitive duration risk.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
60 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (Within the context of the Fund's core markets, the ECB's Survey on the Access to Finance of Enterprises \(SAFE\), covering Q1 2026, shows that euro area firms reported a sharp increase in bank interest rates on loans - with 26% of firms reporting higher rates, up from 12% in the prior quarter - alongside tightening collateral requirements and a persistent financing gap, with SMEs reporting bank loan availability falling short of their needs by a net 3% \(ECB SAFE Survey, Q1 2026\). These conditions directly expand the addressable market for the Fund's strategies in receivables financing and SME lending, where speed, flexibility, and data-driven underwriting continue to offer a compelling alternative to bank credit. The Fund's pipeline remains active and the investment environment continues to favour its differentiated positioning.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
